      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this will count quota used by the current provider as well, and we don\u0027t want to do that since this is supposed to be everything *except* that.  For example, this method is called at the start of doesProviderHaveQuotaForLabel() (which is the real decision point where the launcher decides if it should launch a node or not).  That method starts by calling this method, then substracts self.api.nodes_cache.getQuota(provider, include_requested\u003dTrue), which will include the nodes we\u0027re subtracting here.\n\nThe short version of this comment is I think we should skip the present provider in this loop.\n\nBut there\u0027s another aspect to this: we should consider what\u0027s going on in doesProviderHaveQuotaForLabel().  It includes requested nodes because that method is designed to be nearly up-to-date with the current launcher requests.  In other words, it\u0027s supposed to have as accurate of a quota calculation as we can get.  This method is not designed for that.  It\u0027s designed to move slowly and cache for 5 minutes at a time, and that\u0027s because it\u0027s intended to account only for non-zuul nodes.  We don\u0027t expect those to change quickly (the use case is long-lived servers in the same account, not really another zuul, or even this zuul, doing work in the same account).\n\nI worry that by including the other providers in this method and caching it, that we\u0027ll have some pretty out-of-date info.\n\nThis method is called from 3 locations, all of which then apply the current launcher usage from the node cache.  I think that\u0027s where we should subtract the usage from the other endpoints.  The issue we\u0027re trying to address here is other launcher providers using the same endpoints, and they are likely to be very active.  We should be able to react to their use just as quickly as we react to our own use, by using the node cache to see their nodes and requested nodes.  If we move this calculation there, then we will benefit from it.\n\nSince it\u0027s three call sites, it may make sense to introduce one more general method which does the subtraction of the other providers from the cloud quota, and then have those three call sites call it.  Two of the sites include requested nodes, one of them does not (you can see the comments to understand why).\n\nFinally, there\u0027s one more thing I\u0027d like to consider.  The old code was (at a very high level):\n\nmin(cloud_quota, provider_resource_limits) - usage\n\nThe new code is:\n\nmin(cloud_quota - usage, provider_resource_limits)\n\nThese two are not quite equivalent.  Consider a cloud quota with instances\u003dmath.inf.  Subtract the usage of other providers on the same endpoint from that, and the resulting quota object is still instances\u003dmath.inf.  That means the usage of other endpoints won\u0027t actually be accounted for unless it is something that is metered by the cloud itself.  I chose instances in this example because that is often not metered by the cloud.\n\nSo I think at a high level, this process needs to look like the old method.  Perhaps we need to calculate usage differently.\n\nThe result might be:\n\nmin(cloud_quota, provider_resource_limits) - other_endpoint_usage - this_provider_usage\n\nI think it may make sense to put the min() call back into getProviderLimits as it was before (that method is well-named) and just insert the \"other endpoint usage\" calculation between this method and its 3 call sites.",
      "revId": "9a92b1fdf4ad1efa86d1dac10391e5450c9654be",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543"
